WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.050
Those pieces make four delegation
patterns, each with a cost.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:09.100
They're drawn from Infoblox's documentation
but apply to any comparable platform.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.262
Pattern A: one central platform with
delegated division administrator groups.

4
00:00:14.262 --> 00:00:16.588
You get one database, one audit log,

5
00:00:16.588 --> 00:00:21.987
and one DNSSEC and response-policy posture,
with everything denied by default.

6
00:00:21.987 --> 00:00:27.231
The risks are permission sprawl, first-match
ordering surprises when roles overlap,

7
00:00:27.231 --> 00:00:30.676
the import bypass, and reports
that ignore permissions.

8
00:00:30.676 --> 00:00:34.675
Pattern B: division-owned servers
inside the central platform.

9
00:00:34.675 --> 00:00:38.950
The division gets local resilience
and can restart its own services,

10
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.675
confined to its own servers.

11
00:00:40.675 --> 00:00:45.386
But it can't change platform-wide settings,
and signed zones still have a central

12
00:00:45.386 --> 00:00:47.742
hidden primary, because in NIOS only the

13
00:00:47.742 --> 00:00:50.312
Grid Master can be
primary for a signed zone.

14
00:00:50.312 --> 00:00:54.650
Pattern C: a separate network
view or IP space per division.

15
00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:58.435
It's the only way to hold
overlapping private address space,

16
00:00:58.435 --> 00:01:01.062
and it draws a clean visibility boundary.

17
00:01:01.062 --> 00:01:03.915
It needs dedicated DHCP servers per view,

18
00:01:03.915 --> 00:01:07.187
and uniqueness across
views needs federation.

19
00:01:07.187 --> 00:01:12.626
Pattern D: delegate a zone to DNS
servers the division runs itself.

20
00:01:12.626 --> 00:01:16.237
That's full autonomy,
with any DNS software.

21
00:01:16.237 --> 00:01:18.516
But central loses the audit trail,

22
00:01:18.516 --> 00:01:23.645
the response policy coverage and the
signing of that zone, and keeps only the

23
00:01:23.645 --> 00:01:28.774
parent zone, the delegation records and,
if the child is signed, its DS records.

24
00:01:28.774 --> 00:01:32.620
The patterns combine, and the
worked example uses all four.
